# THE MANAGED REPOSITORIES.
#
# `pnpm sync` clones the other 15 repositories in here and pnpm-workspace.yaml
# binds them into one workspace. They are NOT vendored: vendoring 15
# repositories into a 16th would undo the split this project exists to make.
#
# What IS under version control is repos.json, which pins each repository's
# revision. Without it the composite state of the project would not be
# recorded anywhere and a cross-repository regression could not be bisected.
# See docs/manifest.md.
repos/

# Dependencies
node_modules/
.pnpm-store/

# THE WORKSPACE-ROOT LOCKFILE.
#
# Not committed, because its contents are not a property of THIS repository.
#
# `pnpm-workspace.yaml` binds `repos/*` into this workspace, so a single
# workspace-root lockfile describes all 16 projects at once. `repos/` is
# gitignored (above), which means the file's contents depend on how much of
# `repos/` happened to exist when `pnpm install` last ran:
#
#   repos/ empty (a fresh clone, and CI)  -> 1602 lines, importers: `.`
#   repos/ populated (after `pnpm sync`)  -> 2059 lines, importers: `.` + 15
#
# Measured, not guessed. `pnpm install` rewrites it in whichever direction it
# finds, so whichever version were committed, one of the two documented setup
# steps in docs/workflow.md §2 would leave the working tree dirty — permanently,
# because the next `pnpm install` puts it back. That is a booby trap, not an
# artefact: the diff carries no information anyone can act on, and the pressure
# is always to commit it and move on.
#
# It would also be pinning packages whose SOURCE IS NOT IN THIS REPOSITORY and
# whose resolved versions change every time `pnpm update:manifest` re-pins. The
# things that genuinely need pinning are pinned where they belong:
#
#   - the composite state of the 15 repositories -> repos.json (committed here)
#   - each repository's own dependencies         -> repos/<name>/pnpm-lock.yaml,
#     committed in that repository, by that repository's CI
#
# The alternative — `shared-workspace-lockfile=false`, giving mc-dev-meta's own
# dependencies a root lockfile with no `repos/*` importers — was tried and
# rejected. It makes pnpm write a lockfile INTO each cloned repository, and once
# the managed repositories depend on each other (which is the entire point of
# this workspace) the version it writes there is `link:../mc-kernel`, which is
# meaningful only inside this workspace. Verified: adding a `workspace:*`
# dependency rewrote `repos/mc-noise/pnpm-lock.yaml`. That would leave all 15
# clones dirty, and `pnpm sync` skips dirty repositories — the tool's own safety
# rule firing on damage the tool caused. A trap costing 457 lines is not worth
# trading for one that disables synchronisation entirely.
#
# The cost of this line is that CI cannot use `--frozen-lockfile`. See
# .github/workflows/ci.yaml, which says what that does and does not buy.
pnpm-lock.yaml

